    "summary": "Blazing Scarlet>Fire segue, tight set, Brent's standout keyboards",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a scorching Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain transition and an inspired second‑set flow. Brent Mydland’s analog jams add extra texture while Jerry’s guitar stays hot throughout. Overall energy is high and the setlist highlights several deep cuts that hold up well.",
    "review": "April 13, 1983 at Patrick Field House in Burlington captured a quintessential early‑80s Dead night that still feels fresh. The first set rolls through classic material—Jack Straw, West L.A. Fadeaway, Far From Me—and even the occasional quirky banter from Bob keeps the vibe loose. The second set is where the magic truly ignites: a blistering Scarlet Begonias → Fire on the Mountain segue launches into a soaring Estimated Prophet → Eyes of the World stretch that culminates in an emotional Morning Dew. Brent Mydland’s keyboard work shines, especially during the transition, adding a warm analog groove that many fans cite as among his best for this era. Jerry Garcia’s lead guitar is consistently praised—blazing, inventive, and at times “smoking”—even when some mixes pull his tone low. Phil Lesh provides a solid bass foundation throughout, while Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann lock in a steady dual‑drum pulse. The intimate gym setting amplifies the energy, making this show a must‑listen for anyone hunting a vibrant snapshot of the 1983 tour.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest window into that night, the SBD capture by sacks (gd83-04-13.sbd.sacks.1614.sbeok.shnf) offers crisp balance with only minor volume quirks and an audience splice, delivering the most reliable listening experience.",
